# Make the day's research visible to the newsroom WITHOUT building an image.
#
# WHAT IT REPLACES
# ----------------
# The seam's `repin` stage. The corpus used to be a checksum-pinned tar inside
# the org image, so a new day's research meant a new ~5GB image, a daily build,
# and a daily chance to lose the day to something that is not journalism. It
# did: 2026-09-30 died at the candidate health probe, 2026-10-01 died at the
# deploy gate. This unit is the corpus arriving as DATA instead.
#
# IT NEEDS NO DEPLOY KEY OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT
# --------------------------------------------------------------
# The private remote is `git@github-clank-private:apresmoi/clankandslop-private`
# -- an SSH host alias -- and root's ~/.ssh/config binds that alias to
# /root/.ssh/clank_private with `IdentitiesOnly yes`. So a bare `git fetch` here
# already carries the right identity and nothing else. Verified on this box
# 2026-10-01: the bare fetch exits 0.
#
# Contrast clank-release.service, which fetches the PUBLIC repo whose remote is
# plain `git@github.com:...` with no alias, gets no key from the config, and
# therefore has to name one explicitly. If anyone ever rewrites this remote to a
# bare github.com URL, this unit stops being able to fetch and the newsroom
# quietly stops getting research -- so the alias is load-bearing, not cosmetic.
#
# Note also that the private remote's PUSH url is deliberately
# `DISABLED-the-seam-never-pushes-the-private-repo`. This job reads; it must never
# acquire the ability to write to the corpus it serves.
#
# WHY IT RUNS ON THE HOST AND NOT IN THE CONTAINER
# ------------------------------------------------
# Because the private repo needs a credential and the agents must never hold
# one. `kind: git` would clone INSIDE the agent container, which needs egress
# and a deploy key; that is the isolation model this newsroom is built on and
# it is not negotiable. The credential stays here; what crosses the boundary is
# a directory of files.
#
# IT IS NOT ALLOWED TO PUBLISH A BAD CORPUS
# -----------------------------------------
# The script validates the staged tree before anything becomes visible — the
# same four refusals `repin` made at build time (missing, unparseable,
# incomplete, not this edition's), now made at refresh time — and on any
# finding it drops the staging tree, alarms, and leaves the previous corpus
# exactly where it was. The newsroom's own read-time check then refuses to
# commission against a corpus that is not today's, so a silent stale day is not
# reachable from here.
[Unit]
Description=Refresh the newsroom research corpus volume from the private repo
After=network-online.target docker.service
Wants=network-online.target
OnFailure=clank-alarm@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/root/work/clankandslop
Environment=PATH=/home/clank/deploy-work/node24/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
ExecStart=/home/clank/deploy-work/node24/bin/node /root/work/clankandslop/agentic-org/scripts/corpus-refresh.mjs
TimeoutStartSec=600
